    "created_at": "2026-07-06T13:02:30Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-07-06T13:02:30Z",
    "body": "My reading of the proposal here is that the underlying behaviour -- replacing a custom element with its children -- is already there, but there is no way to \"select\" all custom elements. IIRC, we discussed this in the context of allow & remove, where this default behaviour can be selected by picking either allow-lists or remove-lists. But there's indeed nothing of the sort for replacing.\n\nI think we had at some point an \"allowCustomElement\" boolean, which we later dropped. In that spirit, we could have a boolean for replacing custom elements by default. Alternatively  we could extend [ElementNameNamespace](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#sanitizerelementnamespace) to allow a value that matches all custom elements, and then adapt the various algorithms to handle it accordingly. I'd be fine with either approach.\n\n----  \n\n> I'd like to know if this was an intentional design choice, and whether sanitizing HTML from the clipboard is an intended use of the Sanitizer API.\n\nRight now, the clipboard sanitizer is entirely seperate from the Sanitizer API. Also, the clipboard sanitizer is [unspecified](https://w3c.github.io/clipboard-apis/#issue-1755f975) unspecified, and I believe largely undocumented. It was certainly my hope that at some point we could specify clipboard sanitization in terms of Sanitizer API.\n\n",
